On Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:18:37 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> claude <claude@p7x.dev> writes:
> 
> > One cost to note is that lab-process-mail answers one message per run
> > and daemon(8) waits the full interval after every run. A batch of ten
> > messages therefore takes nine extra minutes at the default of 60. A
> > smaller lab_process_maild_interval shortens that, since a run on an
> > empty inbox costs only a fetch.
> 
> That sucks. It would be much better for lab-process-mail to indicate
> whether it needs to continue. What mechanisms are there available to do
> that? Show code snippets of each approach.

First, a limit that applies to every option. daemon(8) cannot act on
an indication. -r and -R restart the child after a fixed delay
whatever its exit status, and no option keys on the status (daemon(8)
on 15.1). So whichever channel lab-process-mail uses, something
between daemon(8) and lab-process-mail has to read it. That is a short
foreground loop, with no "&" and no wait, which daemon(8) runs with -R
as it runs lab-process-mail in v4 [1]:

    daemon -f -H -o LOG -P PIDFILE -R INTERVAL \
        -t lab-process-maild LOCALBASE/bin/lab-process-maild drain

drain answers messages back to back while lab-process-mail says there
is more, then exits. daemon(8) then owns the sleep, as it does now.
I ran this shape under daemon(8) on this host with a stub that takes
two seconds per pass:

- Three messages were answered back to back, with no delay between
  them.
- A SIGTERM during the restart delay stopped daemon(8) at once.
- A SIGTERM during a pass reached only drain, since daemon(8) signals
  its child and not the group. sh holds a trapped signal until the
  foreground command ends, so the pass finished. drain then exited,
  and daemon(8) did not restart it.

These are the channels lab-process-mail could use.

1. Exit status

lab-process-mail exits 0 once the inbox is empty, 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL in
sysexits(3)) when it answered a message and more is waiting, and 1 on
failure, as it does now. In lab-process-mail, after the notice:

	notify "answered: ${subject} (inbox $(inbox_count))"
	[ "$(inbox_count)" -eq 0 ] || exit 75

In lab-process-maild:

	drain() {
		local status stopping

		stopping=
		trap 'stopping=1' TERM
		while :; do
			status=0
			"${bindir}/lab-process-mail" || status="${?}"
			[ "${status}" -eq 75 ] || exit "${status}"
			[ -z "${stopping}" ] || exit 0
		done
	}

The status is a channel only the program sets, read by the process
that ran it, with nothing to store or clean up. A run by hand that
clears the inbox still exits 0. The cost is one non-zero status that
is not a failure. That needs documenting in lab-process-mail, and a
caller that treats any non-zero status as an error, such as
"just process-email", would need to accept 75.

A failure still ends drain, and daemon(8) runs it again after the
interval. A stuck message then fails at once, before the agent
starts, as it does in v4.

2. A file it writes

lab-process-mail writes what is left, and drain reads it:

	# lab-process-mail
	inbox_count > "${HOME}/var/db/${PROGNAME}/pending"

	# lab-process-maild drain
	while "${bindir}/lab-process-mail" &&
	    [ "$(cat "${HOME}/var/db/lab-process-mail/pending")" -gt 0 ] &&
	    [ -z "${stopping}" ]; do
		:
	done

This carries the same bit as option 1 through a second channel. Both
programs must agree on the path, and a run that dies before writing
the file leaves the last count behind, so drain can act on a stale
value. A file is the right tool when a turn returns data. One bit
fits in the exit status.

3. Ask the inbox

lab-process-mail says nothing, and drain checks the fact itself. This
is the check v3's loop made:

	while "${bindir}/lab-process-mail" &&
	    [ "$(notmuch count tag:inbox)" -gt 0 ] &&
	    [ -z "${stopping}" ]; do
		:
	done

It reads a fact the program does not author, and it needs no
protocol. The cost is that lab-process-maild must know notmuch, its
environment, and what counts as work. That is lab-process-mail's
knowledge, and the two drift apart if that definition changes, for
example if a held message were left in the inbox. It is also not
lab-process-mail indicating anything, which is what you asked for.

4. Standard output

lab-process-mail prints "more" or "done" and drain parses it. I would
not do this. daemon(8) sends the same stdout to the log, so the
signal shares a channel with the diagnostics, and it is the parsed
control string that CLAUDE.md's "Own the loop" warns against, even
though this script is deterministic.

5. Exec itself again

lab-process-mail continues by replacing itself, so daemon(8) -R stays
as in v4 and needs no drain:

	notify "answered: ${subject} (inbox $(inbox_count))"
	[ "$(inbox_count)" -eq 0 ] || [ -n "${stopping}" ] ||
	    exec "${bindir}/${PROGNAME}"

Each pass gets a fresh process, but this is the internal loop you
asked to remove, in the form of a tail call. A run by hand drains the
whole inbox again, and the program decides when it runs again.

I recommend option 1. It is the conventional way for a one-shot Unix
program to report its outcome, and the program stays free of any loop
or timing. The loop lives in lab-process-maild, which you said in the
RFC thread should "loop and do the work, and sleep if there are no
inbox messages" [2]. daemon(8) keeps the sleep, the log, the pidfile,
and the signals. The drain loop is about ten lines. It needs no
background process, because the stop behavior relies only on sh
holding a trapped signal.

If you pick one, v5 will carry it. It would be one patch for the exit
status and one for drain and its man page entry, in place of v4's
reliance on -R alone.

